{"name":"Quiver","hex":"#886037","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Quiver","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A stream in Illinois, United States; in full, Quiver Creek.","A township in Mason County, Illinois, United States, named after Quiver Creek."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Quiver"}],"entries":[{"title":"Quiver","blocks":[{"text":"A quiver is a container for holding arrows or bolts. It can be carried on an archer's body, the bow, or the ground, depending on the type of shooting and the archer's personal preference. Quivers were traditionally made of leather, wood, furs, and other natural materials, but are now often made of metal or plastic."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Wenceslas_Hollar_-_Quivers_and_hunting_horns.jpg/330px-Wenceslas_Hollar_-_Quivers_and_hunting_horns.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quiver","wikidata":"Q222560"},{"title":"Quiver (mathematics)","blocks":[{"text":"In mathematics, especially representation theory, a quiver is another name for a multidigraph; that is, a directed graph where loops and multiple arrows between two vertices are allowed. Quivers are commonly used in representation theory: a representation V of a quiver assigns a vector space V(x) to each vertex x of the quiver and a linear map V(a) to each arrow a."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quiver_(mathematics)","wikidata":"Q493980"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Bayeux_Tapestry_scene51_Battle_of_Hastings_Norman_knights_and_archers.jpg/500px-Bayeux_Tapestry_scene51_Battle_of_Hastings_Norman_knights_and_archers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Bayeux_Tapestry_scene51_Battle_of_Hastings_Norman_knights_and_archers.jpg/1280px-Bayeux_Tapestry_scene51_B